\name{peptide_mz}
\alias{peptide_mz}
\title{The m/z of intact peptides at a given charge}
\usage{
peptide_mz(peptides, charge)
}
\arguments{
\item{peptides}{Rows of a \code{digest$peptides} data.frame.}

\item{charge}{Total charge, a positive whole number.}

}
\description{
Two conventions are handled explicitly, because getting either wrong is invisible in the
answer.
}
\details{
\strong{The proton mass, not the hydrogen atom.} 1.00727646677 against 1.007825 - a difference of
0.55 mDa, or 1.1 ppm at m/z 500, which on an Orbitrap is a match versus a miss.

\strong{Permanently charged modifications are not double-counted.} Trimethylation of a lysine
epsilon-amine gives a quaternary ammonium, which carries its charge with no proton. UniProt
records the delta as 43.054227 (C3H7 *minus an electron*); mzLib loads it one proton lighter, at
42.046951 (C3H6), so adding \code{charge} protons to \code{monoisotopic_mass} gives the right m/z. Adding a
full complement to UniProt's mass would put a 2+ trimethylated peptide half a Thomson high.

\code{fixed_charges} is deprecated and always 0 from the pyMzLib 0.7.0 bridge, so the formula below
adds \code{charge} protons. It still subtracts \code{fixed_charges}, which keeps the m/z right for a digest
from an older bridge, whose \code{monoisotopic_mass} carried UniProt's charged mass: the m/z is the
same from either.

Fragments carry \code{neutral_mass}, charged modifications included at their corrected mass, so a
fragment's m/z at charge z is \code{(neutral_mass + z * 1.00727646677) / z}.
}
\value{
A numeric vector of m/z, one per row.
}
\examples{

\dontshow{.mzlibr_example <- mzLibR:::replay_bridge_start()}
digest <- peptidoform_fragments("P02768", max_modifications = 1)
peptide_mz(digest$peptides, charge = 2)
\dontshow{mzLibR:::replay_bridge_stop(.mzlibr_example)}
}
